The '''Women’s Prize for Non-Fiction''' is a prize for [[non-fiction]] writing by women, a sister prize to the [[Women's Prize for Fiction]]. It was announced in February 2023 and was first awarded in 2024, for books published in 2023. Its main prize of £30,000 will be funded for three years by the [[Charlotte Aitken Trust]], and the winner will also receive a statuette named the Charlotte.<ref>{{cite web |title=Seeking Sponsors for Women's Non-Fiction |url=https://www.womensprizeforfiction.co.uk/features/features/news/seeking-sponsors-for-womens-non-fiction |website=Women's Prize for Fiction |access-date=8 February 2023 |date=8 February 2023 |archive-date=8 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230208091221/https://www.womensprizeforfiction.co.uk/features/features/news/seeking-sponsors-for-womens-non-fiction |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=green>{{cite news |last=Green |first=Alex |title=Kate Mosse: New prize celebrating non-fiction by women will amplify and honour |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/nonfiction-liz-truss-rishi-sunak-b2277973.html |access-date=8 February 2023 |work=The Independent |date=8 February 2023 |language=en |archive-date=8 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230208091219/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/nonfiction-liz-truss-rishi-sunak-b2277973.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="knight">{{cite news |last1=Knight |first1=Lucy |title=‘Big books by blokes about battles’: why we need the Women’s prize for nonfiction |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2023/feb/10/why-we-need-womens-prize-for-nonfiction |access-date=11 February 2023 |work=The Guardian |date=10 February 2023}}</ref> It was announced in June 2023 that the inaugural sponsor of the prize would be the family tree company [[Findmypast]].<ref>{{cite web |last1= |first1= |title=Findmypast inaugural sponsor for Women's Prize for Non-Fiction |url=https://www.womensprizeforfiction.co.uk/features/features/news/findmypast-inaugural-sponsor-for-womens-prize-for-non-fiction |website=Women's Prize for Fiction |access-date=16 February 2024 |date=7 June 2023 |archive-date=16 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240216000244/https://www.womensprizeforfiction.co.uk/features/features/news/findmypast-inaugural-sponsor-for-womens-prize-for-non-fiction |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Kate Mosse]], announcing the launch of the prize, said it was "not about taking the spotlight away from the brilliant male writers, it's about adding the women in."<ref name="shaffi">{{cite news |last1=Shaffi |first1=Sarah |title=Women’s prize to launch annual award for women’s non-fiction writing |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2023/feb/08/womens-prize-to-launch-annual-award-for-womens-non-fiction-writing |access-date=8 February 2023 |work=The Guardian |date=8 February 2023}}</ref>

The prize entry criteria state that: "Any woman writing in English – whatever her nationality, country of residence, age or subject matter – is eligible. Books must be published in the United Kingdom between 1 April in the year the Prize calls for entries, and 31 March the following year, when the Prize is announced. Books from all narrative non-fiction categories intended for general readership, commonly known as ‘trade non-fiction’, are encouraged, and the judging panel considers the titles submitted using the guiding tenets of excellence, originality and accessibility."<ref name=enter>{{cite web |title=Enter the Women's Prize for Non-Fiction |url=https://womensprizeforfiction.co.uk/enter-the-womens-prize-for-non-fiction |website=Women's Prize for Fiction |access-date=30 December 2023 |archive-date=29 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231229234639/https://womensprizeforfiction.co.uk/enter-the-womens-prize-for-non-fiction |url-status=live }}</ref> 

Five women make up the panel of judges.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Women’s Prize For Non-Fiction |url=https://womensprize.com/prizes/womens-prize-for-non-fiction/ |access-date=2026-08-07 |website=Women's Prize |language=en-US}}</ref> The 2024 judges were historian [[Suzannah Lipscomb]]  (chair), fair fashion campaigner [[Venetia La Manna]], academic, author and consultant, Professor [[Nicola Rollock]], biographer and journalist [[Anne Sebba]], and author and 2018 winner of the [[Women's Prize for Fiction]] [[Kamila Shamsie]].<ref name="nonfiction2024" />

The first longlist for the Women's Prize for Non-Fiction, of 16 titles,  was announced on 15 February 2024.<ref name="longlist2024">{{cite web |title=Women’s Prize For Non Fiction |url=https://womensprize.com/prizes/womens-prize-for-non-fiction/ |website=Women's Prize |access-date=15 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240215235333/https://womensprize.com/prizes/womens-prize-for-non-fiction/ |archive-date=15 February 2024 |quote=The longlist has been announced}}</ref> ''[[The Guardian]]'' noted that the subject matter of the longlisted books included "Capitalism, artificial intelligence, Renaissance history and motherhood."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Creamer |first1=Ella |title=Guardian writer and Observer critic longlisted for inaugural Women’s prize for nonfiction |url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2024/feb/15/womens-prize-nonfiction-guardian-observer |access-date=4 March 2024 |work=The Guardian |date=15 February 2024}}</ref> The shortlist was announced on 27 March 2024 and the winner on 13 June 2024.<ref name="nonfiction2024">{{cite web |title=Women's Prize for Non-Fiction 2024 |url=https://womensprize.com/prizes/womens-prize-for-non-fiction/ |website=Women's Prize |access-date=30 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231230000150/https://womensprize.com/prizes/womens-prize-for-non-fiction/ | archive-date=30 December 2023}}</ref>

The 2025 judges are journalist, author & broadcaster [[Kavita Puri]] (chair), writer & broadcaster [[Leah Broad]], novelist & critic [[Elizabeth Buchan]], writer & environmental academic Dr [[Elizabeth-Jane Burnett]], and author of the newsletter ''The Hyphen'', [[Emma Gannon]].

The 2025 longlist of 16 titles was announced in February 2025;  the announcement said that "readers will find agenda-setting reportage on contemporary issues alongside revisionist histories and myth-busting biographies; memoirs of self-determination and intimate narratives that shine a light on ordinary people combine with real-life criminal cases, notorious and forgotten, whilst a handful of the books defy genre-classification, weaving multiple disciplines into one compelling narrative work."<ref>{{cite web |last1= |first1= |title=Announcing the 2025 Women's Prize for Non-Fiction longlist! |url=https://womensprize.com/announcing-the-2025-womens-prize-for-non-fiction-longlist/ |website=Women's Prize |access-date=24 February 2025 |date=12 February 2025}}</ref>

